<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cleon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 422 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cleon was an Athenian politician who dominated the assembly after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a> in 429 BCE and pressed for vigorous war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>. In 425 BCE he captured the Spartan troops on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sphacteria">Sphacteria</a>, near Pylos. He was killed in 422 BCE at Amphipolis, in the same battle as the Spartan general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brasidas">Brasidas</a>, and is portrayed harshly by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristophanes">Aristophanes</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Cleon was the son of Cleaenetus, a wealthy tanner, and was among the first prominent Athenian politicians of the 5th century BCE to gain power from outside the established elite. He made his name in the late 430s BCE by opposing the strategy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a>, in particular his policy of avoiding battle with the invading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-league">Peloponnesian League</a> in 431 BCE. In 430 BCE he led the opposition to Pericles and accused him of mismanaging public funds; Pericles was removed from office but quickly reinstated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a> died of the plague in 429 BCE, Cleon rose quickly, and by 428 BCE, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a>, he was the most persuasive speaker in the Athenian Assembly. He won support among poorer citizens by raising the pay for jury service, on which many lived, and by using the courts against rivals. In 424 BCE he was elected one of the ten strategoi.</p><p>After Mytilene revolted in 428 BCE and surrendered, Cleon proposed putting to death all its adult men and enslaving the women and children. Diodotus spoke against him and narrowly won, though about 1,000 of the rebellion's leaders were still executed. In the spring of 425 BCE an Athenian fleet defeated a Spartan one at Pylos and trapped 400 Spartan soldiers on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sphacteria">Sphacteria</a>. Spurred on by Cleon, the Athenians turned down Spartan peace offers. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> dragged on, the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicias">Nicias</a> offered to give up his command, and Cleon accepted, promising to take the island in 20 days. He did so at the Battle of Sphacteria, although the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demosthenes-general">Demosthenes</a> is often credited with the tactical success.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 422 BCE Cleon led a force to retake Amphipolis, which had been lost to the Spartans. He was outmanoeuvred by the Spartan general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brasidas">Brasidas</a> at the Battle of Amphipolis and killed by a peltast as his army was routed. Brasidas also died in the battle, removing the chief opponents of peace on both sides, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-nicias">Peace of Nicias</a> was signed in 421 BCE.</p><p>The two main contemporary sources, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristophanes">Aristophanes</a>, portray him harshly, often as a demagogue. Aristophanes had a grievance, since Cleon is said to have complained about his lost play <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a>, and Thucydides was exiled after failing to hold Amphipolis. Some modern scholars see bias in both writers, while others argue that the portrait was less shaped by personal dislike than once believed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cleon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The knights were a byword for wealth and privilege: in Aristophanes’s comedy The Knights, staged in 424 BCE, they form the chorus that attacks the popular leader <b>Cleon</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/athens">Social Hierarchy of Classical Athens</a></li><li><blockquote>Brasidas captured it in 424 BCE; both he and the Athenian <b>Cleon</b> died in battle there in 422 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/amphipolis">Amphipolis</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in Amphipolis, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.98,23.73&amp;year=-432" aria-label="Athens on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5141 798 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Cleon"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5591 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5591 1068h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.98,23.73&amp;year=-432">What was here in 432 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
